===Alternative forms===
* {{l|en|oyes}}

===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|xno|-}} and {{bor|en|frm|oyez}}, {{m|fro|oiez}}, imperative plural of {{m|fro|oir||to hear}}, from {{der|en|fro|oïr||to hear}}, itself from {{der|en|la|audiō|audiō, audīre|to hear}} ({{m|en|oyez}} in particular corresponding to the second-person plural imperative form, {{m|la|audite}}). Commonly folk-etymologized as (and pronounced homophonously to) {{m|en|O}} + {{m|en|yes}} in the early modern period.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/ˈəʊˌjeɪ/|lang=en}}, {{IPA|/oʊˈjeɪ/|lang=en}}, {{IPA|/oʊˈjɛs/|lang=en}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|/oʊˈjeɪ/|lang=en}}<!--homophonous with "oh yay!"-->
* {{hyphenation|o|yez|lang=en}}
{{rfap||UK|lang=en}}

===Interjection===
{{en-interj}}

# [[hear ye|Hear ye]]. Attend. (Called by public [[crier]]s or in [[court]] usually three times to secure silence and/or attentiveness).

====Usage notes====
* It is still used in the United States Supreme Court, similar to calling "order", and in many state supreme courts, though some lower courts have dropped its use.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|es}}

# A cry of "oyez".

===Verb===
{{en-verb|es}}

# to [[proclaim]] with a cry of “oyez”
#* '''1599''', {{w|Thomas Nashe}}, ''Nashes Lenten Stuffe'':
#*: I truſte you make no queſtion about thoſe dull pated pennifathers, that in ſuch dudgen ſcorne reiected him, drunck deep of the ſowre cup of repentance for it, when the high flight of his lines in common brute was '''ooyeſſed'''.

